cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  Pushing Four Raspberry Pis to the Memory Wall: Bit-Exact 30B
  Mixture-of-Experts Inference, +16% over the Public Record
authors:
  - family-names: "Correa Villa"
    given-names: "Daniel"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-05-21"
license: MIT
url: "https://github.com/danielcorrea-hellomatik/distributed-llama"
repository-code: "https://github.com/danielcorrea-hellomatik/distributed-llama"
keywords:
  - distributed-inference
  - edge-llm
  - raspberry-pi-5
  - mixture-of-experts
  - tensor-parallelism
  - arm-cortex-a76
  - bit-exact-optimization
abstract: >-
  A production-grade distributed inference cluster running Qwen3-30B-A3B
  (Mixture of Experts, Q40) at 15.143 tok/s decode (+16.1% over the public
  ceiling of 13.04 tok/s; b4rtaz #255) and 14.449 tok/s end-to-end sustained
  on 4x Raspberry Pi 5 16GB, built on a patched fork of distributed-llama
  v0.16.5. The work documents twelve source-level changes, ARM-specific
  compiler flags, kernel/network tuning and a WFE/SEV barrier, all
  validated bit-exact (SHA-256 of deterministic output) against the
  unoptimised baseline, with n=20 benchmarks and 95% confidence
  intervals. It also catalogues the failure modes (frameworks and
  techniques tried and rejected with measurements) that define the
  practical ceiling of CPU-only MoE inference on ARM SBC hardware.
